e time on a GPU cluster.\n3. Includes the theoretical bubble analysis and analytical throughput computation (feasible without GPUs).\n\nNow let me structure the plan according to the researcher's request:\n\nStep 1 — Repository inspection: report findings (empty workspace, no code). Provide theoretical background for the methods and their canonical locations in known open-source codebases (since the actual repo is absent, mark as assumption that the canonical implementations will be used). Provide the microbatch → bubble relationship:\n- 1F1B: bubble fraction = (p-1)/(m + p - 1) (for t_b = 2 t_f); monotonic decrease with m; throughput → max as m → ∞.\n- 1F1B-I (interleaved / ZB-VPP with k virtual stages): requires m ≡ 0 (mod k*p) for fully zero bubble; bubble fraction roughly (p-1)/(k*m ...) — monotonic decrease in steps.\n- ZB-1p: zero bubble when m ≡ 0 (mod p) (m a multiple of p). For non-multiples, small residual bubble. So throughput is at its maximum for all multiples of p — flat at max once m ≥ p, not strictly increasing past that; the \"improves with microbatches\" claim holds in the regime m < p (fill/drain dominated).\n- ZB-2p: zero bubble for arbitrary m (any m ≥ 1... actually any m), at the cost of ~ small extra compute (two passes); throughput flat/high across all m.\n\nFor the design:\nIndependent variable: number of microbatches m. Levels: must cover m < p, m = p, m = k p. E.g., with p = 8 (assuming an 8-stage pipeline on 8 GPUs), m ∈ {2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64} — includes m < p (2, 4), m = p (8), m > p multiples (16, 32, 64). Also maybe non-multiples (e.g., 12) to test ZB-1p residual bubble. Actually to distinguish ZB-1p at multiples vs non-multiples, include both: m ∈ {2, 4, 6, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32}. Hmm.\n\nWait — but there's a subtlety: the \"1F1B-I\" interleaved schedule. In interleaved 1F1B, you usually need m divisible by p (or by k*p where k = number of chunks). So for a fair comparison, we might set m as multiples of p for all methods to keep validity.