gn computes them from schedule definitions. That's honest.\n\nNow, the analytical model (feasibility without GPU): I'll write a design for a pure-Python CPU simulation/closed-form:\n- Function `bubble_fraction(schedule, p, m, tau_f, tau_b)` returning analytic β per schedule.\n- Function `expected_throughput(β, p, m, units)` = m·N_tokens_per_mb/(T) where T = (3m+p−1)τ_f for 1F1B etc.\n- For ZB-1p/ZB-2p, since we don't have the exact schedule code, either (i) use the paper's published closed forms, or (ii) implement a schedule simulator that constructs the F/B assignment per stage per time slot and measures idle — a mock schedule simulator is fully implementable on CPU. Mark the schedule-construction formulas as an assumption/requires-source.\n\nActually for a cleaner deliverable: the analytic model's main purpose is to verify the *shape* predictions (monotone for 1F1B/1F1B-I; plateau for ZB-1p at multiples; flat for ZB-2p) and to compute the expected bubble fractions for chosen (p, m) so the measured values can be compared. This is doable.\n\nI should also give the GPU-run spec: launch commands, config flags, steps:\n- Install DeepSpeed (with Zero-Bubble schedule support) or use the paper's official code (Qi et al.), plus Megatron for 1F1B-I. Actually many tasks use Megatron-LM for interleaved 1F1B and DeepSpeed for ZB. Simpler: DeepSpeed Pipeline Engine with zero_bubble mode and schedule flags, and Megatron-style interleaved for 1F1B-I. I'll present config spec generically.\n\nLet me now also define measurement protocol concretely:\n- Warmup: run 10 optimizer steps discarded (or until measured step-time stabilizes within ±1% for 3 consecutive steps).\n- Steady-state measurement window: 50 optimizer steps per (method, m) rep; record per-step wall time via CUDA events.\n- Throughput = (m · microbatch_bs · seq_len) tokens per step / mean step_time = tokens/sec. Also samples/sec = m·microbatch_bs / step_time.\n- Report mean ± std over ≥5 reps.\n\nRepeats & randomization